{"title":"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Casework;_Using_Answer_Choices)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Casework; Using Answer Choices)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1.1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1.1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_1.2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1.2</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Note</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Related_Problems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Related Problems</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The faces of each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> standard dice are labeled with the integers from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be the probabilities that when all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> dice are rolled, the sum of the numbers on the top faces is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. What other sum occurs with the same probability as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/d/0ade5d80c9703a12e0a4b63c0216ded4cc263767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)} \\text{ 13} \\qquad \\textbf{(B)} \\text{ 26} \\qquad \\textbf{(C)} \\text{ 32} \\qquad \\textbf{(D)} \\text{ 39} \\qquad \\textbf{(E)} \\text{ 42}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"386\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Casework.3B_Using_Answer_Choices.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Casework;_Using_Answer_Choices)\">Solution 1 (Casework; Using Answer Choices)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Casework; Using Answer Choices)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The number 10 can be achieved by \\( 4+6 \\), \\( 5+5 \\), \\( 6+4 \\), in which there must exist at least 5 number ones. \n</p><p>Thus, we can write an answer in the form \\( 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + x + y \\), where \\( x + y \\) must sum to 5. We notice that \\( x + y = 5 \\) can be either \\( 1 + 4 \\), \\( 2 + 3 \\), \\( 3 + 2 \\), or \\( 4 + 1 \\). So \\( x + y = 4\\) cases.\n</p><p>The question now is which of the following answer choices can be written as a 5 term consecutive sum with the last two numbers \\( x\\) and \\(y\\) having exactly 4 cases.\n</p><p>We first take E. 42. We see that we can have 5 consecutive number 6's to get 30. We then have a remaining 12, which can be represented as 6 + 6 only, not giving us our wanted 4 cases. \n</p><p>We try D. 39. We see we can write this as 5 consecutive number 6's. This gives us a remainder of 9, which can be written as 6+3, 4+5, 5+4, or 3+6, giving us 4 cases, exactly like the cases we got for 10.\n</p><p>Our answer is therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/c/31c9b74d5345edf4ec513a28db40b604a61e6da9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)} \\text{ 39}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"63\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1.1\">Solution 1.1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1.1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>If you wish to check your answer.\n</p><p>32 = a consecutive number 5's or 6's, gives a remainder of 7 and 2 respectively.\n</p><p>The consecutive number of 6's obviously doesn't work.\n</p><p>The consecutive number of 5's gives the remainder 7, which is \\( 1+6 \\), \\( 2 + 5 \\), \\( 3+4 \\), \\( 4+3 \\), \\( 5+2 \\), \\( 6+1 \\), which is 6 cases and not 4.\n</p><p>26 is either consecutive 5's or 4's. \n</p><p>This gives a remainder of 1 or 6. 1 obviously doesn't work. 6 is \\( 1+5 \\), \\( 2+4 \\), \\( 3+3 \\), \\( 4+2 \\), or \\( 5+1 \\), which is 5 cases, not 4.\n</p><p>13 is consecutive 2's or 1's, giving a remainder of 3 and 7 respectively. \n</p><p>We see that 3 doesn't work, and we already know that 7 doesn't work either.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1.2\">Solution 1.2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1.2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>A dice has the property all sums are symmetrically distributed (as the dice is fair). Therefore, one can realize that a sum of 10 (3 more than the minimum sum 7) comes the same as 3 less than the maximal sum 42, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/d/1fd830d446f41bf02b20f14671ad1532f36f2518.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{39}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p><p>Minor edits by Frogflick\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>It can be seen that the probability of rolling the smallest number possible is the same as the probability of rolling the largest number possible, the probability of rolling the second smallest number possible is the same as the probability of rolling the second largest number possible, and so on. This is because the number of ways to add a certain number of ones to an assortment of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ones is the same as the number of ways to take away a certain number of ones from an assortment of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s.\n</p><p>So, we can match up the values to find the sum with the same probability as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. We can start by noticing that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the smallest possible roll and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> is the largest possible roll. The pairs with the same probability are as follows:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/e/40e7a9427b93fcc8f03df01c70490668cc80de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(7, 42), (8, 41), (9, 40), (10, 39), (11, 38)...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"311\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>However, we need to find the number that matches up with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. So, we can stop at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/d/33d161420cd8df4cff596dd188e1f1e8a5d2361f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(10, 39)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" /> and deduce that the sum with equal probability as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/5/e058d401a1b942f106f081ff1fcca8a8b1d42a7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$39$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" />. So, the correct answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/0/270eb4fe4a255d40ebfa6fb4e7c022f44b5f656f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)} \\text{39}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"57\" height=\"28\" />, and we are done.\n</p><p>Written By: Archimedes15\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Add-on by ike.chen: to see how the number of ways to roll <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/5/e058d401a1b942f106f081ff1fcca8a8b1d42a7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$39$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> are the same, consider this argument:\n</p><p>Each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> dice needs to have a nonnegative value; it follows that the number of ways to roll <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/3/b9312d23241da92eee9864bfb10576effec3a373.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom {10-1}{7-1}=84$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"120\" height=\"53\" /> by stars and bars. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/a/0ea7c9bccd0fe3c94d33f6a2c4ccbb93a5b51707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10-7=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"82\" height=\"13\" />, so there's no chance that any dice has a value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/9/f09822df35793025120c76e53e6704ae3a87510d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&gt;6$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Now imagine <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> piles with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blocks each. The number of ways to take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blocks away (making the sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/7/267ebb7a0db5f55fc493ac4d075b7e52a37a9a02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7\\cdot 6-3=39$\" width=\"104\" height=\"12\" />) is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/f/8bfbcee9caecbd92e305610f56654b2f80178470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom {3+7-1}{7-1}=84$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"142\" height=\"53\" />.\n</p><p>correction to the add-on: the die need a positive value. We first give every die 1 so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/a/0ea7c9bccd0fe3c94d33f6a2c4ccbb93a5b51707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10-7=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"82\" height=\"13\" /> \"balls\" left to put into the 7 more bins/die. a dice can have a value of 0 for the # of added balls. Thus, from stars and bars it follows that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/7/db7a312eb7afc454c8fa90e9babf1e8a296a2cfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{7+3-1}{7-1}=84$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"144\" height=\"55\" /> ways\n</p><p>~mathboy282\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let's call the unknown value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. By symmetry, we realize that the difference between 10 and the minimum value of the rolls is equal to the difference between the maximum and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. So, \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/4/9c4b302574891066d6d2a19b7c6bdfefc128bf33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10 - 7 = 42- x$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"124\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/0/2a0bf6ec4f82d68799314c52a724058e8e22b7de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 39$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" /> and our answer is  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/c/31c9b74d5345edf4ec513a28db40b604a61e6da9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)} \\text{ 39}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"63\" height=\"28\" />\nBy: Soccer_JAMS\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For the sums to have equal probability, the average sum of both sets of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> dies has to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/b/deb397750a7e6849fe12f2e2293e5b0c7746683d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6+1)\\cdot 7 = 49$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"118\" height=\"18\" />. Since having <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is similar to not having <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, you just subtract 10 from the expected total sum. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/f/81f2fd72f55e69704c04aace346483bd7d347690.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$49 - 10 = 39$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"100\" height=\"13\" /> so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/c/31c9b74d5345edf4ec513a28db40b604a61e6da9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)} \\text{ 39}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"63\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>By: epicmonster\n</p><p>Revised solution by Williamgolly (includes bijections):\nNotice that we first must have at least a 1 on each die.  Now, we form the following bijection: biject each value on the original die, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> to a value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/5/1f50ad524e676579df070adcb5377c8c3c494a01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7-x$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> on the new die. Notice how now we need to 'take away' 3 from seven dies that all show 6.  Therefore, the answer is 39.\n</p><p>Note: This is basically just having <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> on the tops of the dice, and asking about the sum of the bottom of the dice. The opposite sides of a dice add up to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. ~Fib_0112358\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The expected value of the sums of the die rolls is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/a/26a3d3effe4aa51a162affe19772a5ff1e688da9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3.5\\cdot7=24.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"101\" height=\"13\" />, and since the probabilities should be distributed symmetrically on both sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/b/9bbab57580b74c71d1e10bbebf35f67a3dcb677e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"31\" height=\"13\" />, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/8/db8a8fa2a9fc97470fa13e41cbab5bb401e9432b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24.5+(24.5-10)=39$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"186\" height=\"20\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/c/31c9b74d5345edf4ec513a28db40b604a61e6da9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)} \\text{ 39}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"63\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>By: dajeff\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6\">Solution 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Another faster and easier way of doing this, without using almost any math at all, is realizing that the possible sums are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/9/1396794b12e471659f90a8db515c22d442a5fadf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${7,8,9,10,...,39,40,41,42}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"197\" height=\"16\" />. By symmetry, (and doing a few similar problems in the past), you can realize that the probability of obtaining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the same as the probability of obtaining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/2/992d7e0a1895bd91121075c07c3b428b39fccfef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(8)=P(41)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" /> and on and on and on. This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/1/1415423289a1465d458cc35a31f5a8d3b4ed5ecf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(10)=P(39)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" />, and thus the correct answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/c/31c9b74d5345edf4ec513a28db40b604a61e6da9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)} \\text{ 39}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"63\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>By: fhdsaukfaioifk\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Note\">Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Calculating the probability of getting a sum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is also easy. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cases:\n</p><p><br />\nCase <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/e/24e74cdca9e11e8563c9cd7c1d4ece94e7ae897e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,1,1,1,1,1,4\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/d/d9d1a69ec60c72b7b25556e7917c82b61c3372db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${7 \\choose 6}=7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"70\" height=\"53\" /> cases\n</p><p><br />\nCase <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/3/753fbd9698292a55f6390c4b769bda028b7febb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,1,1,1,1,2,3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/1/501aedbd42b80084d96823a1f8cd3fafa5fc31f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${7 \\choose 5}=6 \\cdot 7=42$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"134\" height=\"53\" /> cases\n</p><p><br />\nCase <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/3/d33142249c31cfa1baf403b7ae25a49f3fc3176f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,1,1,1,2,2,2\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/f/dafc90ecad9e020e8057fc89bc26401d87bd264b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${7 \\choose 4}=\\frac {7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5}{3 \\cdot 2}=35$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"160\" height=\"53\" /> cases\n</p><p><br />\nThe probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/3/a7391f82cb5b59bc71408586b9e6e47d1aad8501.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${84 \\over 6^7} = \\frac{14}{6^6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"70\" height=\"38\" />. \n</p><p>Calculating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/f/7bf8cf167990d3d4a27df8cb8a70727fd8fb6067.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6^6$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" />: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/7/847a0986aa3ce734e71864d410610f7528ec71df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6^6=(6^3)^2=216^2=46656$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"209\" height=\"21\" /> \n</p><p>Therefore, the probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/4/8f4c19467c4ced24026678499b53f1cb69612fb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${14 \\over 46656} = \\boxed{{7 \\over 23328}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"134\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>~Zeric Hang (Main writer) and fhdsaukfaioifk (Editor)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Related_Problems\">Related Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Related Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There is similar to problem 11 of the AMC 10A in the same year, which is almost a replica of the problem mentioned by Zeric Hang in the Note section:\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11\">https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/odHniGWWLvw\">https://youtu.be/odHniGWWLvw</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B\">2018 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 8\">Problem 8</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 10\">Problem 10</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 18\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 24\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"2018 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 25\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_10_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 10 Problems and Solutions\"> All AMC 10 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017205317\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.052 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.071 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 505/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 2322/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 156/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 13700/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   12.910      1 -total\n 59.91%    7.734      1 Template:AMC10_box\n 39.11%    5.049      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:16019:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017205317 and revision id 288184. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}